// Fairy Tale: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== fairy-tale.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Ring In The Salt
genre: fairy-tale + family
tone: Bright, grave and crystalline
era: - | period
setting: A mountain kingdom with no salt, and the mines under a far country | fictional | otherworld
logline: A princess sent to marry a king whose people have no salt throws her ring down a mine shaft in her father's country as her only dowry, and must then follow it underground, on foot, the whole width of the world.
premise: In the kingdom beyond the mountains meat rots, bread is flat and children are pale; they have gold, and trade it by weight for salt. Their young king asks for a foreign princess. Her father offers gold and jewels. {kinga} says her new people have gold. She asks for one salt mine instead, and drops her betrothal ring into its deepest shaft, which everyone takes for a girl's foolishness.
synopsis: The first act is a bargain nobody understands. {kinga} is practical, not dreamy: she has seen a salt ledger and knows what a kingdom is worth. The old miner {gaffer_wit}, who alone was watching when the ring fell, tells her that salt runs under the mountains like a river, but that nobody has ever followed it. She travels to her wedding by the road above, and arrives to a court that is kind and hungry and a husband, {boleslav}, who is shy and has never tasted a pickled cucumber. The second act is the descent. On her wedding night she asks leave to go and fetch her dowry. With {gaffer_wit}, a lamp, and a goat that will not be left behind, she goes down the shaft in her father's country. The underworld of the tale is geological and strange: a lake no one has crossed, where the boat is a hollowed crystal; a chamber of sleeping miners turned to salt, who can be woken with a drop of fresh water and ask what year it is; the Treasurer, {the_treasurer}, a spirit of the mine as a grey man with a ledger, who owns everything below and trades only like for like. He will let the salt follow her if she pays in something she cannot get back. Not her ring; she has already given that. He takes her memory of her mother's voice. She goes on, lighter and sadder, and the vein follows her under the mountains like a hound, cracking the dark behind her. In the third act she comes up inside her husband's country, in a field, and tells his baffled men to dig. They strike something hard. The first block of salt is split, and in it is the ring. The feast that follows is the first seasoned meal the kingdom has ever eaten, and the camera attends closely to faces. {boleslav} asks what it cost her. She says she cannot remember, which is true. That night {gaffer_wit}, who heard the queen her mother sing once, long ago, hums her a tune. It is not the memory. It is something.
question: What is the difference between a treasure and a gift?
themes: practical love; a ruler's wealth measured by what the people can eat; prices that cannot be refunded
audience: Families; children from seven; adults who like a fairy tale told straight
viewpoint: With {kinga}; a storyteller's voice only at the very beginning and end
stakes: A kingdom's health, a marriage begun as a treaty, and a part of herself
conflict: A princess with one idea against the width of the earth and a spirit who gives nothing for nothing
ending: The salt follows her; the ring is found in the first block; the kingdom eats. She has lost her mother's voice, and is hummed a tune.
experience: Wonder underground, the satisfaction of a riddle answered by walking, and a happy ending with a small ache in it
look: White crystal caverns lit by one lamp; a grey spirit with a ledger; green fields above; salt glittering on black bread
sound: Dripping water, a pick, a goat's bell echoing. A hummed tune without words.
m: create | nature | self-vs-others | bittersweet
@ kinga | Princess Kinga | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 17 | Salt for her new people | She has seen children who have never had enough | The salt is under the wrong country | Practical, stubborn, kind without softness | From a bride with an odd request to a queen who paid for it | Plain, direct, asks prices | Travelling cloak, a ring on a cord, stout boots
@ gaffer_wit | Gaffer Wit | co-lead | mentor | human | he/him | 70 | To see where the vein goes before he dies | He has wondered for fifty years | His knees | Dry, curious, superstitious on principle | From the one who saw the ring fall to the one who hums the tune | Miner's sayings | Leather cap, a tallow lamp, a pick worn short
@ boleslav | King Boleslav | supporting | love-interest | human | he/him | 19 | To deserve the stranger he has married | He is a shy king of a pale people | She leaves on the wedding night | Gentle, earnest, braver than he sounds | From baffled bridegroom to the first to dig | Careful, formal, blushes | A crown slightly too big, a wooden spoon
@ the_treasurer | The Treasurer | supporting | antagonist | supernatural | he/him | - | The books balanced | Nothing has ever left his mine unpaid for | She has already given him her ring | Courteous, exact, without malice | From refusing to keeping his bargain to the grain | Like a clerk reading totals | Grey skin like rock salt, a ledger, crystal spectacles
@ the_goat | The Goat | featured | ally | animal | it | - | To come too | Nobody asked it | It licks the walls | Stubborn, sure-footed | From nuisance to finding the way across the lake | A bell | A white nanny with one black ear
~ kinga - gaffer_wit | princess and miner | a foolish girl by a shaft | a queen he hums to
~ kinga - boleslav | bride and bridegroom | a treaty | a meal shared
~ kinga - the_treasurer | traveller and spirit of the mine | a refusal | a bargain kept on both sides
# The Dowry | EXT | Mine Head, Her Father's Country | DAY | kinga, gaffer_wit | Throw the ring | Chests of gold refused. One mine asked for. She drops her ring down the deepest shaft and listens. Only the old miner counts how long it falls | The ring goes into the salt | 5 | crowd=80, props=ring+gold chests
# The Dowry | INT | Great Hall, Mountain Kingdom | NIGHT | kinga, boleslav | Taste the wedding feast | Roast meat with no savour, flat bread, pale children at the door. He is kind. She asks leave to fetch her dowry | {kinga} decides to go under | 5 | crowd=150, props=feast
# Below | INT | Mine, Deepest Gallery | NIGHT | kinga, gaffer_wit, the_goat | Go down | A lamp, a ladder, a goat that follows. Walls that glitter. A vein running east like a white road | | 5 | animals=goat, vfx=salt caverns
# Below | INT | Underground Lake | NIGHT | kinga, gaffer_wit, the_goat | Cross | Black water no one has crossed. A boat of hollowed crystal. Sleeping miners of salt on the far shore; one wakes at a drop of fresh water and asks the year | | 6 | water, animals=goat, vfx=crystal boat+salt sleepers
# Below | INT | Treasurer's Chamber | NIGHT | kinga, the_treasurer, gaffer_wit | Bargain | A grey man with a ledger. Like for like. Something she cannot get back. He names it: her mother's voice. She pays | She gives up the memory | 7 | vfx=salt spirit, props=ledger
# Below | INT | Tunnel Under The Mountains | NIGHT | kinga, gaffer_wit, the_goat | Be followed | She walks. Behind her the rock cracks and whitens: the vein coming after like a hound | The salt follows her | 5 | animals=goat, vfx=moving salt vein
# The Field | EXT | Field, Mountain Kingdom | DAY | kinga, boleslav, gaffer_wit | Dig here | She comes up in a meadow, grey with dust. The king takes the first spade. A hard white block. Split, it has a ring in it | The ring is found | 6 | crowd=200, props=salt block+ring
# The Field | INT | Great Hall, Mountain Kingdom | NIGHT | kinga, boleslav, gaffer_wit, the_goat | Eat | Salt on black bread. Faces. He asks what it cost. She cannot remember. By the fire the old miner hums something | | 5 | crowd=150, animals=goat, props=feast
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from the Polish legend of a princess's ring found in a salt mine, freely retold
